msgid ":mod:`pwnlib.elf.corefile` --- Core Files"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile:1
msgid "Read information from Core Dumps."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile:3
msgid ""
"Core dumps are extremely useful when writing exploits, even outside of "
"the normal act of debugging things."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile:7
msgid "Using Corefiles to Automate Exploitation"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile:9
msgid ""
"For example, if you have a trivial buffer overflow and don't want to open"
" up a debugger or calculate offsets, you can use a generated core dump to"
" extract the relevant information."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile:59 pwnlib.elf.elf:45
msgid "Module Members"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:1
msgid "Bases: :py:class:`~pwnlib.elf.elf.ELF`"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:1
msgid ""
"Enhances the information available about a corefile (which is an "
"extension of the ELF format) by permitting extraction of information "
"about the mapped data segments, and register state."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:5
msgid ""
"Registers can be accessed directly, e.g. via ``core_obj.eax`` and "
"enumerated via :data:`Corefile.registers`."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:8
msgid ""
"Memory can be accessed directly via :meth:`pwnlib.elf.elf.ELF.read` or "
":meth:`pwnlib.elf.elf.ELF.write`, and also via "
":meth:`pwnlib.elf.elf.ELF.pack` or :meth:`pwnlib.elf.elf.ELF.unpack` or "
"even :meth:`.string`."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:11
msgid ""
"Path to the core file.  Alternately, may be a :class:`.process` instance,"
" and the core file will be located automatically."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:38
msgid "Mappings can be iterated in order via :attr:`Corefile.mappings`."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:61
msgid ""
"Let's build an example binary which should eat ``R0=0xdeadbeef`` and "
"``PC=0xcafebabe``."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:64
msgid ""
"If we run the binary and then wait for it to exit, we can get its core "
"file."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:77
msgid ""
"You can specify a full path a la ``Corefile('/path/to/core')``, but you "
"can also just access the :attr:`.process.corefile` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:80
msgid ""
"There's a lot of behind-the-scenes logic to locate the corefile for a "
"given process, but it's all handled transparently by Pwntools."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:85
msgid ""
"The core file has a :attr:`exe` property, which is a :class:`.Mapping` "
"object.  Each mapping can be accessed with virtual addresses via "
"subscript, or contents can be examined via the :attr:`.Mapping.data` "
"attribute."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:94
msgid ""
"The core file also has registers which can be accessed directly. Pseudo-"
"registers :attr:`pc` and :attr:`sp` are available on all architectures, "
"to make writing architecture-agnostic code more simple. If this were an "
"amd64 corefile, we could access e.g. ``core.rax``."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:106
msgid ""
"We may not always know which signal caused the core dump, or what address"
" caused a segmentation fault.  Instead of accessing registers directly, "
"we can also extract this information from the core dump via "
":attr:`fault_addr` and :attr:`signal`."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:111
msgid ""
"On QEMU-generated core dumps, this information is unavailable, so we "
"substitute the value of PC.  In our example, that's correct anyway."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:119
msgid ""
"Core files can also be generated from running processes. This requires "
"GDB to be installed, and can only be done with native processes. Getting "
"a \"complete\" corefile requires GDB 7.11 or better."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:132
msgid ""
"The process is still running, but accessing its :attr:`.process.corefile`"
" property automatically invokes GDB to attach and dump a corefile."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:138
msgid ""
"The corefile can be inspected and read from, and even exposes various "
"mappings"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:145
msgid ""
"It also supports all of the features of :class:`ELF`, so you can "
":meth:`pwnlib.elf.elf.ELF.read` or :meth:`pwnlib.elf.elf.ELF.write` or "
"even the helpers like :meth:`pwnlib.elf.elf.ELF.pack` or "
":meth:`pwnlib.elf.elf.ELF.unpack`."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:148
msgid "Don't forget to call :meth:`.ELF.save` to save the changes to disk."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:155
msgid "Let's re-load it as a new :attr:`Corefile` object and have a look!"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:161
msgid "Various other mappings are available by name, for the first segment of:"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:163
msgid ":attr:`.exe` the executable"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:164
msgid ":attr:`.libc` the loaded libc, if any"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:165
msgid ":attr:`.stack` the stack mapping"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:166
msgid ":attr:`.vvar`"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:167
msgid ":attr:`.vdso`"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:168
msgid ":attr:`.vsyscall`"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:170
msgid ""
"On Linux, 32-bit Intel binaries should have a VDSO section via "
":attr:`vdso`. Since our ELF is statically linked, there is no libc which "
"gets mapped."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:177
msgid ""
"But if we dump a corefile from a dynamically-linked binary, the "
":attr:`.libc` will be loaded."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:183
msgid ""
"The corefile also contains a :attr:`.stack` property, which gives us "
"direct access to the stack contents.  On Linux, the very top of the stack"
" should contain two pointer-widths of NULL bytes, preceded by the NULL- "
"terminated path to the executable (as passed via the first arg to "
"``execve``)."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:191
msgid ""
"When creating a process, the kernel puts the absolute path of the binary "
"and some padding bytes at the end of the stack.  We can look at those by "
"looking at ``core.stack.data``."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:199
msgid ""
"We can also directly access the environment variables and arguments, via "
":attr:`.argc`, :attr:`.argv`, and :attr:`.env`."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:215
msgid "Corefiles can also be pulled from remote machines via SSH!"
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:228
msgid "Make sure fault_addr synthesis works for amd64 on ret."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:237
msgid ""
"Corefile.getenv() works correctly, even if the environment variable's "
"value contains embedded '='. Corefile is able to find the stack, even if "
"the stack pointer doesn't point at the stack."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:252
msgid ""
"context.disable_corefiles disables the automatic corefile generation for "
"crashed processes. For running processes, io.corefile still invoke GDB to"
" generate a coredump."
msgstr ""

#: of pwnlib.elf.corefile.Corefile:268
msgid ""
"Corefile gracefully handles the stack being filled with garbage, "
"including argc / argv / envp being overwritten."
msgstr ""

